    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64741,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 332,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Upper vagina (most commonly but not exclusively) {17592267}</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T16:56:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64742,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 332,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tubulosquamous polyps are typically an incidental finding in patients within a wide age range (39&ndash;78&nbsp;years in the only reported series) {17592267}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T16:56:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64743,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 332,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There are insufficient epidemiological data.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T16:56:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64744,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 332,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T16:56:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64745,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 332,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It has been suggested that these lesions are derived from misplaced periurethral Skene glands, the female equivalent of prostatic glands in males, given their positivity for prostatic markers {17592267; 27180063; 21979599}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T16:56:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64746,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 332,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are polypoid, small (1&ndash;3&nbsp;cm) lesions, with a predominantly solid cut surface, sometimes with small cysts {17592267}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T16:56:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64747,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 332,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Tubulosquamous polyps are covered by squamous epithelium. Within the core of the polyp, nests of bland squamous epithelium are present, often with abundant glycogenation and central keratinous debris. Around the periphery of the squamous nests, small tubules (sometimes with a double layer of cells) are present; these typically constitute a minor component. Tubules not associated with squamous epithelium are sometimes present. The surrounding stroma is hypocellular and collagenous or oedematous, sometimes with prominent vessels {17592267}. Goblet cells, sebaceous glands, hair follicle&ndash;like structures, and a basaloid epithelial appearance may occasionally be seen {17592267; 21804389; 19851205}.</p>\r\n<p>Immunohistochemically, the inner layer of the tubules is typically positive with prostatic markers (PSA, PAP, and NKX3-1). However, staining with these markers may be very focal or even negative. The squamous elements are typically positive with p63 and GATA3 {17592267; 21979599; 27180063}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-22T12:23:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64748,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 332,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T16:56:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
